1: # Source-moduli loops: exact E7 marked-node parameter and local transport
2: 
3: Working record after P3F.51. The exact marked source and local closure
4: claims in SM.1--3 and SM.5 are proved as P3F.54 after root's full
5: independent audit. No return class for a source-moduli loop
6: is assigned here. The scalar-return results retain their specified
7: fixed source and parameter. The purpose of this note is to identify
8: the additional actual transport problem, using literal coordinate
9: maps.
10: 
11: ## SM.1. The complete selected-node cover for the E7 marked form
12: 
13: Use the full F51 source
14: \[
15:  y^2=P(x,t,r)=x^3+3rx^2+tx+1,\qquad G=(0,1).
16: \]
17: A singular finite fibre has a double root \(u\ne0\). The equations
18: \(P(u,t,r)=P_x(u,t,r)=0\) give the exact maps
19: \[
20:  r(u)=\frac{1-2u^3}{3u^2},\qquad
21:  t_0(u)=u^2-\frac2u,\qquad
22:  P(x,t_0(u),r(u))=(x-u)^2(x+u^{-2}).
23:  \tag{SM.1}
24: \]
25: Conversely these displayed functions give every marked finite
26: singular fibre of the normalized E7 source. A double root
27: determines \(u\), so this is its normalization parameter, with
28: no unmarked choice of a discriminant branch.
29: 
30: The selected fibre is nodal exactly when \(u^3\ne-1\). At
31: \(u^3=-1\) the displayed polynomial has a triple root, and
32: the selected fibre is the type II cusp. Thus the explicit
33: parameter for the marked source together with a selected
34: whole-zero \(I_1\) is
35: \[
36:                          U=\mathbb C^*\setminus\{u:u^3=-1\}.
37:  \tag{SM.2}
38: \]
39: The other finite fibres are allowed to coalesce to the II
40: fibre of F51 while the selected one stays nodal.
41: For example \(r=1,u=1/2\) selects the separate nodal
42: fibre \(t_0=-15/4\), whereas \(u=-1\) selects the cusp.
43: The maps (SM.1), not an equality of fibre profiles, distinguish
44: these two situations.
45: 
46: Their derivatives are
47: \[
48:  r'(u)=-\frac{2(1+u^3)}{3u^3},\qquad
49:  t_0'(u)=\frac{2(1+u^3)}{u^2}.
50:  \tag{SM.3}
51: \]
52: In particular the selected-node normalization is ramified
53: over a cusp parameter of the \(r\)-line.
54: 
55: The remaining constant marked coordinate equivalence is also
56: explicit. For \(s^3=1\), the map
57: \[
58:  x=s^2X,\quad y=s^3Y=Y,\quad t=s^4T
59: \]
60: sends \(r\) to \(r/s^2\) and \(u\) to \(u/s^2\), while
61: retaining \(G=(0,1)\). Hence its finite quotient on (SM.2)
62: is described by \(u^3\in\mathbb C^*\setminus\{-1\}\).
63: No monodromy of the total threefold is inferred from this
64: finite source-coordinate quotient.
65: 
66: ## SM.2. Actual local extension in the permitted source parameters
67: 
68: Fix a point of \(U\), its selected zero and a proved E7
69: datum whose only cap is at \(III^*\). Over a sufficiently small disc in
70: \(U\), retain the displayed functions \(r(u),t_0(u)\) and
71: the actual section \(G\). The finite Weierstrass total source
72: is smooth at a nodal or cuspidal fibre: at a singular point
73: of the cubic, its derivative with respect to \(t\) is the
74: nonzero root \(x\). Thus the source family is a submersion
75: to this small parameter disc. At infinity the full root
76: equations of F51, their relative quotient charts and their
77: actual marked section resolutions give the simultaneous
78: minimal-source map.
79: 
80: The cap division section is holomorphic in this parameter
81: disc. Indeed it is the inverse branch of multiplication by
82: the cap order at the recorded central point; on a reduced
83: root both the identity group and component norm are the
84: ones already checked in F41. Every finite-character
85: averaging is the same explicit finite sum, and its remaining
86: unit has a holomorphic logarithm after shrinking the disc.
87: Its analytic root therefore gives the literal family of
88: commuting norm lifts. The Cartier correspondence of F51
89: uses the same invariant ideals in these relative charts.
90: 
91: At the selected node the exact holomorphic Morse chart is
92: the one in SM.3 below; at a point of \(U\) its square-root
93: branches are fixed on the small disc. The chosen fan and
94: the physical line character therefore have fixed marked
95: charts. Their monomial face maps and all cap annular maps
96: remain holomorphic in the source parameter. A fixed
97: sufficiently small scalar works uniformly on the compact
98: closure of this disc, by the strict norm estimate on the
99: common ordinary complement. The resulting glued family
100: is proper and is a holomorphic submersion on that disc.
101: 
102: Consequently the return around the boundary of such a
103: disc is isotopic to the identity by the actual smooth
104: bundle over the disc: choose a smooth horizontal complement
105: to the vertical tangent bundle and lift radial paths from
106: its centre. Properness makes these lifts complete on the
107: closed disc, giving a smooth fibre trivialization there.
108: Its restriction contracts the boundary return. This
109: includes a collision of two
110: unselected nodal fibres to a type II fibre: the source
111: surface remains smooth, the selected nodal chart persists,
112: and the starred cap maps remain the same full-coefficient
113: maps. It does not include a puncture \(u^3=-1\) of (SM.2),
114: where the selected zero itself ceases to be nodal.
115: This local extension keeps the finite source fibres unmodified.
116: The joint \(III^*+II\) cap belongs to the locus \(r^3=1\);
117: no extension of its II cap across a deformation to two
118: separate nodal fibres is asserted here.
119: 
120: ## SM.3. The exact nodal-coordinate transport at a selected cusp
121: 
122: Put \(X=x-u\), \(q=t-t_0(u)\), and
123: \[
124:                          a(u)=u+u^{-2}.
125: \]
126: In the actual smooth source near its selected singular
127: point the equation becomes
128: \[
129:  y^2=X^2(X+a(u))+q(X+u),\qquad
130:  q=\frac{y^2-X^2(X+a(u))}{X+u}.
131:  \tag{SM.4}
132: \]
133: For \(a(u)\ne0\), choose a small chart
134: \(|X|<c|a(u)|\), with \(c<1\), and choose the displayed
135: square roots. Define
136: \[
137:  \xi=\frac{y-X\sqrt{X+a(u)}}{\sqrt{X+u}},\qquad
138:  \eta=\frac{y+X\sqrt{X+a(u)}}{\sqrt{X+u}}.
139:  \tag{SM.5}
140: \]
141: These are actual holomorphic coordinates near \(X=y=0\):
142: their Jacobian in \((X,y)\) is
143: \(-2\sqrt{a(u)}/u\), which is nonzero. Their product is
144: exactly \(q\), so (SM.5) is a base-preserving node chart.
145: 
146: At \(u_*\) with \(u_*^3=-1\), \(a'(u_*)=3\). On a small
147: positive loop about \(u_*\), the square root of \(X+a(u)\)
148: changes sign, while that of \(X+u\) can be retained on a
149: single analytic branch. The exact final chart map is
150: \[
151:                               (\xi,\eta)\longmapsto(\eta,\xi).
152:  \tag{SM.6}
153: \]
154: Thus the two normalization branches of the selected nodal
155: fibre are exchanged. Their ordered Picard gluing map is
156: reversed: an identification \(L_{p_+}\to L_{p_-}\) is
157: replaced by its inverse \(L_{p_-}\to L_{p_+}\). This is an
158: explicit source and line-branch transport, not yet the
159: clutch of the completed threefold.
160: 
161: ## SM.4. Exact remaining comparison
162: 
163: Historical status of the first draft, retained verbatim. SM.5 below
164: now supplies the Tate/Cartier transition and marked double-cover
165: closure. The smooth relative return and the other global punctures
166: remain open.
167: 
168: For a global loop in \(U\), the source and cap data must
169: be transported together with the ordered node branches,
170: the source Picard character, the period marking and the
171: chosen periodic fan. Formula (SM.6) is the specific
172: transition at the three selected-cusp punctures. To close
173: a family over such a loop one must extend its resulting
174: torus map over the actual chosen toric completion, and
175: retain the finite-cap line lifts and their annular maps.
176: 
177: Those full toric/cap endpoint maps have not been computed
178: in this note. In particular no return in \(\Theta_7\) is
179: assigned from the equality of the fixed-source scalar
180: formulas, from the two-branch exchange alone, or from
181: divisibility of a known scalar-return integer. The
182: punctures \(u=0,\infty\) also require their actual
183: source-coordinate and completion comparison. This is
184: the separate source-moduli transport problem left by
185: the scalar-scope audit.
186: 
187: ## SM.5. Exact Tate, Cartier-line and fan transition
188: 
189: The source-line transition in SM.3 can be identified on the
190: actual Tate model, retaining the line frame. In the selected
191: node chart let \(\Delta(t,r)\) denote the discriminant
192: polynomial (S51.3), without its factor 16. The exact identities
193: are
194: \[
195:  f(t_0)=-a(u)^2/3,\qquad
196:  \Delta_t(t_0)=-4u\,a(u)^3,\qquad
197:  j=-\frac{64a(u)^3}{u\,q}+O(1).
198:  \tag{SM.7}
199: \]
200: The actual Tate parameter \(q_T\), obtained from the inverse
201: of its \(j\)-series, is consequently
202: \[
203:                 q_T=-\frac{u}{64a(u)^3}q+O(q^2).
204:  \tag{SM.8}
205: \]
206: For a sufficiently small fixed loop about \(u_*\), choose the
207: base collar with \(|q|\) uniformly small relative to \(|a|^3\).
208: Then \(q_T/q\) is an invertible holomorphic base function
209: throughout that collar.
210: 
211: The Tate Weierstrass model has \(c_4\) a unit with value one.
212: The source has \(c_4=16a^2+O(q)\). Its actual marked
213: Weierstrass scaling is therefore \(h=2\sqrt a\) times a
214: holomorphic unit with a fixed fourth-root branch. On one
215: positive loop about \(u_*\), that unit has no monodromy and
216: \(h\) changes sign. The source coordinate isomorphism
217: \(x=h^2x_T+\text{translation},y=h^3y_T\), in short form,
218: therefore changes by the marked elliptic inversion.
219: Since the zero section is fixed, there is no translation
220: factor. In the multiplicative Tate coordinate this is
221: literally \(Z\mapsto Z^{-1}\), with \(q_T\) unchanged.
222: The normalized period has the retained change \(-3\),
223: as follows also from (SM.8).
224: 
225: Here is its line map. Write \(P\) for the actual multiplicative
226: point of \(aG\). The line \(\mathcal O(aG-O)\) is represented by
227: \[
228:        (Z,V)\sim(q_T Z,P V).
229:  \tag{SM.9}
230: \]
231: The convergent Tate theta product has the exact identities
232: \[
233:  \Theta(q_TZ)=-Z^{-1}\Theta(Z),\qquad
234:  \Theta(Z^{-1})=-Z^{-1}\Theta(Z).
235:  \tag{SM.10}
236: \]
237: Its ratio \(f_P(Z)=\Theta(Z/P)/\Theta(Z)\) has divisor
238: \((P)-(O)\) and multiplier \(P\). It identifies (SM.9)
239: with the actual source Cartier line and its canonical
240: meromorphic section. On the continued chart \(P'=P^{-1}\),
241: and
242: \[
243:  f_{P^{-1}}(Z^{-1})=P f_P(Z).
244:  \tag{SM.11}
245: \]
246: Thus the divisor-compatible line transition is exactly
247: \((Z,V)\mapsto(Z^{-1},PV)\). Reindex once by the new
248: Tate deck \((Z',V')\mapsto(q_TZ',P^{-1}V')\).
249: The two line factors cancel, giving the literal
250: node-annulus transition
251: \[
252:                 (Z,V,q_T)\longmapsto(q_T/Z,V,q_T).
253:  \tag{SM.12}
254: \]
255: Its square is the identity. The root of \(a\) exchanges
256: the two local branches; every finite multiple of \(G\)
257: avoids the critical point at the limiting cusp because a
258: section cannot meet a critical point of the elliptic
259: map. Hence this Cartier frame extends across that local
260: source neighbourhood. No component-supported line shear
261: has been inserted.
262: 
263: The source translation and its Hom lift are also transported:
264: their Tate form is
265: \((Z,V)\mapsto(g^{-1}Z,\lambda q C\,V)\), with actual
266: nonzero base units \(g,C\). Equation (SM.12) changes
267: \(g\) to \(g^{-1}\) and leaves its scalar factor fixed.
268: It intertwines the old and continued suspension maps
269: literally. The \(III^*\) cap data have holomorphic
270: coefficients on the full small \(u\)-disc, including
271: \(u_*\); their division section, unit root and finite
272: character can be chosen there by the exact constructions
273: of F41/F51. Their annular comparison remains part of
274: this transport, rather than being replaced by a different
275: cap.
276: 
277: On the cone-height lattice (SM.12) is
278: \[
279:              (v_Z,v_V,h)\longmapsto(-v_Z+h,v_V,h).
280:  \tag{SM.13}
281: \]
282: Its action on edge directions is
283: \(J=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)\). It admits no invariant
284: index-one unimodular periodic three-edge triangulation,
285: even allowing an affine translation. Indeed the three
286: unoriented primitive edge directions have the three
287: distinct nonzero reductions in \(\mathbb F_2^2\).
288: Since \(J\) is the identity modulo two, an invariant
289: triangulation would require it to preserve each edge
290: line separately. A reflection has only two eigenspaces,
291: whereas these are three distinct lines.
292: 
293: Thus one loop about this selected-cusp puncture does not
294: close the chosen one-vertex toroidal completion under this
295: specified marked source, line and annular transport. Its
296: second iterate has the identity lattice and Cartier-line
297: transition (SM.12), and the finite cap coefficient
298: charts return as above. Using these literal transition maps
299: in the construction gives the marked holomorphic family
300: on the double cover \(a=\rho^2\). The actual return of that
301: closed double source loop remains the smooth relative
302: transport problem described next. An identity endpoint
303: coordinate transition alone does not compute a
304: mapping-class return.
305: 
306: ## SM.6. The exact weighted source chart on the double cover
307: 
308: Set \(a(u)=\rho^2\), with \(u(\rho)\) the inverse branch
309: through \(u_*\), and use the literal maps
310: \[
311:               X=\rho^2\widetilde X,\quad
312:               y=\rho^3\widetilde Y,\quad
313:               q=\rho^6Q.
314:  \tag{SM.14}
315: \]
316: Equation (SM.4), divided by \(\rho^6\), becomes
317: \[
318:  \widetilde Y^2=\widetilde X^2(\widetilde X+1)
319:                   +Q\bigl(u(\rho)+\rho^2\widetilde X\bigr).
320:  \tag{SM.15}
321: \]
322: Its central source chart is
323: \(\widetilde Y^2=\widetilde X^3+\widetilde X^2+u_*Q\).
324: The discriminant is
325: \(-u_*Q(4+27u_*Q)\), and its compact minimal
326: Weierstrass model has a \(II^*\) fibre at infinity and
327: two \(I_1\) fibres at the displayed finite values.
328: The scalar source factor has become
329: \(\lambda\rho^6Q\), with its actual sixth winding
330: retained.
331: 
332: Every finite section multiple \(aG\) tends to \(O\)
333: in this chart. For example at \(u_*=-1\), the limiting
334: cuspidal fibre is \(y^2=(x+1)^3\); its smooth-group
335: coordinate is \(1/s\) in \(x+1=s^2,y=s^3\).
336: Thus \(aG\) has \(s=1/a\), and (SM.14) sends it to
337: infinity with formal parameter of order \(\rho\).
338: This is an actual section map. It does not by itself
339: identify the complete fixed-sphere trace or its line
340: with a compact page on that limiting source.
341: 
342: The remaining step is the relative smooth map from the
343: actual source-loop exterior and its moving toroidal
344: collar to this weighted chart, including the induced
345: circle line and boundary framing. Its Pontryagin and
346: line-square pairings have not been assigned here.
347: 
348: The expanded exact certificate
349: code/part3/source_moduli_nodes.py verifies (SM.1), (SM.3),
350: (SM.4)--(SM.8), the Tate-deck and suspension intertwining
351: in (SM.12), its square, the cone-height reflection, all six
352: unimodular mod-two edge bases, and (SM.14)--(SM.15).
353: Its algebraic checks pass; no source-loop index is encoded.
354: 
355: Root's independent full SM.5 audit PASS verifies the inverse-j
356: coefficient, square-root Weierstrass scaling, canonical theta
357: Cartier map, exact node reindex cancellation, actual suspension
358: and cap transport, and the mod-two fan obstruction. The export
359: of F54 contains only SM.1--3 and SM.5. SM.4 and SM.6 remain
360: working comparisons; the closed double-loop return is unassigned.
